proof: aaru | opensuse | node-floppy
date: 2026-07-15
lane/VM: test-openSUSE (Leap 16.0, autologin Plasma X11 -> seat0), OBS repo
user groups: "gmipf wheel" (NO cdrom/disk/plugdev) -> groupless
device: NEC USB UF000x floppy (USB-UFI 0409:0040) -> /dev/sda (media present)
mechanism: packaged udev rule /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70-aaru-floppy.rules
  (ENV{ID_TYPE}=="floppy", TAG+="uaccess"); NO cap needed.

Verified: openSUSE systemd 70-uaccess.rules has NO floppy clause (grep count 0), so
our rule is the sole uaccess source for the floppy. uaccess tag is sticky in the udev
db, so RED/GREEN uses a FRESH USB enumeration (detach/attach); ACL polled (~2s lag).

--- RED (no floppy rule, fresh enumeration) ---
  CURRENT_TAGS=:...(no uaccess)... ; ACL ABSENT ; dd /dev/sda -> DENIED

--- GREEN (ONLY 70-aaru-floppy.rules present, fresh enumeration) ---
  node=/dev/sda ; ACL present @~2s (user:gmipf:rw-) ; tag=uaccess ; dd=OK

VERDICT: PROVEN. aaru's packaged floppy rule alone grants the floppy node uaccess to a
groupless user on a fresh plug; without any floppy rule the fresh device is denied.
